div.p:
and your lovely laughter—which truly has set
the heart in my breast a-flutter;
for when I look on you a moment, then can I
no longer speak;

but my tongue is broken, and at once
a subtle fire has run beneath my flesh;
with my eyes I see nothing, and my ears
are ringing;

I say that, of changes in number, not those alone adorn the style which, singular in form, are found on consideration to be plural in force; for instance: "Straightway a countless host,
spreading out along the shores after the tunny, raised a shout";
but those rather are worthy of remark, where there are cases in which the plurals fall with a grander ring and impress us by the very multitude of the number.
